[
    {
        "text": "A hard fork occurs when you want to introduce changes to the software that make older nodes obsolete. The network is effectively fragmented into two parts, one made up of old nodes and one made up of upgraded nodes. The two can't communicate. For all intents and purposes, there are now two networks. Soft forks are upgrades to the network that are backward compatible. Certain upgrades can be added in a way that doesn't interfere with the regular functioning of the network. For instance, a block size reduction. A soft fork might be preferential in scenarios where you don't want to divide users into different camps. In effect, the upgrade is considered opt in. Certain newer features might be invisible to you, but you can still communicate with the upgraded nodes.",
        "start": 480,
        "end": 42000,
        "confidence": 0.9872876,
        "channel": null,
        "speaker": "A",
